<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=airship">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Airship</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1852">1852</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Dirigible, Dirigible balloon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An airship is a lighter-than-air aircraft that can navigate under its own power, using a lifting gas less dense than air. In 1852 Henri Giffard became the first person to make an engine-powered flight, flying 27 km in a steam-powered airship. In 1884 Charles Renard and Arthur Constantin Krebs flew La <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, an electric-motor airship that covered 8 km in 23 minutes and landed where it took off. Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-zeppelin">Ferdinand von Zeppelin</a>'s first rigid airship, LZ1, flew in July 1900. Use declined after the Hindenburg exploded on 6 May 1937, killing 35 of the 97 people aboard.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/airship.jpg" alt="Airship" width="330" height="220" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AngMoKio · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Zeppellin_NT_amk.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the end of the century Stuttgart had become a centre of engineering: Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach built petrol engines in Cannstatt, Robert Bosch opened his workshop in Stuttgart in 1886, and Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin flew his first rigid <b>airship</b> over Lake Constance in 1900.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1811 King Frederick I of Württemberg merged it with the neighbouring Hofen priory to create Friedrichshafen, later the home of the Zeppelin <b>airships</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-buchhorn">Free Imperial City of Buchhorn</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1852</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Henri Giffard</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>In 1784 Jean-Pierre Blanchard fitted a hand-powered propeller to a balloon, the first recorded means of propulsion carried aloft, but not an engine-powered flight.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/steam-engine">Steam engine<span>1606</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/hot-air-balloon">Hot-air balloon<span>1783</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=airship">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
